  "text": "Neither solution is convincing, and I don't think either final answer is right.\n\n**Solution 1 (p = 2)** applies the div-curl lemma to the pairs (a, b) and (c, −b), which is a valid pairing. But the lemma only gives the rigidity direction for p ≥ 2. The flexibility claim below L² is asserted through convex integration without any construction, so the threshold is not derived.\n\n**Solution 2 (p = 4/3)** has correct arithmetic. With 1/p* = 1/p − 1/2, the condition 1/p* + 1/p ≤ 1 gives 2/p ≤ 3/2, so p ≥ 4/3. But the reduction to \"products of a strongly convergent and a weakly convergent factor\" is heuristic. The Sobolev pairing is chosen to match the answer, and the link to the Jacobian threshold n²/(n+1) is an analogy, not a proof. The problem concerns a nonlinear rank-one constraint, not the plain determinant.\n\nI can't check the literature here, and my recollection of the specific source for this threshold is weak. Since neither argument establishes its exponent, I won't pick either one.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
